  "verdict_reason": "Its own run backs the claim: encode 14x-99x and decode 6x-59x faster than hex across 32 B-1 MiB, and formatting 37x faster than per-byte std formatting at 32 bytes, rising above 100x from 256 bytes. The to_slice variants, where hex avoids allocation, show smaller 7.7x-16x encode gaps.",
  "evidence_tier": "A",
  "tier_reason": "Public cargo bench code with the CPU and rustc nightly stated; first-party.",
  "claimed_speedup": "encode 7.06 vs 102.11 ns (32 B) to 30,716.17 vs 3,048,496.30 ns (1 MiB), 14x-99x; decode 17.57 vs 107.13 ns to 108,937.41 vs 6,409,293.90 ns, 6.1x-59x; encode_to_slice 1.57 vs 12.11 ns to 32,631.90 vs 518,929.65 ns, 7.7x-16x; format vs std 10.01 vs 373.63 ns (32 B), 26.68 vs 2,885.37 ns (256 B), 78,056.40 vs 12,348,138.40 ns (1 MiB) (ratios derived from page data)",
  "workload": "libtest #[bench] on 32 B, 256 B, 2 KiB, 16 KiB, 128 KiB and 1 MiB inputs: allocating encode/decode, encode/decode into slices, and Display formatting into a Vec.",
